-- Infobulles anglaises (Beta 1.16) : mêmes clés et mêmes valeurs chiffrées que src/tips.lua ; les libellés cités
-- sont ceux de l'interface anglaise (src/lang_en).
return {
 settings={
  -- Beta 1.42: tow, dirty air, end of race, tyres, strategy, failures and AI sanctions.
  towBreakEnabled='In the race, on a long straight, the leading AI that feels a car in its tow makes a single move to take it away, within the usual lateral limits. Never under flags, against a blue flag, against a car alongside or under braking.',
  towBreakRange='Distance behind the AI within which a car in its wake counts as being in the tow (30 m by default).',
  towBreakStraight='Minimum length of straight ahead of the AI to break the tow (400 m by default): never when approaching a corner.',
  dirtyAirEnabled='Behind a car, in corners, the AI loses downforce: more small line corrections (the human line variation is raised) and a slight lift. Never under flags, in the pit lane or at the start.',
  dirtyAirGap='Distance behind a car below which dirty air applies (20 m by default).',
  dirtyAirLiftPct='Share of throttle lifted in corners in dirty air (3 % by default; 0: line corrections only).',
  finalLapsEnabled='In the last laps of a race, the AI becomes more aggressive and defends its place more firmly. Never against a lapped car, under blue, yellow, VSC or Safety Car.',
  finalLaps='Number of laps at the end of the race concerned (3 by default).',
  finalAggressionPct='Aggression added in these laps (15 % by default), bounded by the maximum aggression.',
  finalDefensePct='Stronger defence in these laps: longer trigger distance and shorter rest (40 % by default).',
  outLapEnabled='After a real pit exit, the AI drives slower while its tyres warm up, until the line or until they reach their ideal temperature. Never at the race start.',
  outLapLevelPct='Level drop during the out-lap (7 % by default; 5 to 10 % is realistic).',
  lockWearEnabled='Wheel lock-ups from driving mistakes become more frequent as the tyres wear, especially with uneven front wear; each lock-up wears that tyre a little more (flat spot). Needs driving mistakes (Personality).',
  lockWearGain='Strength of the link between wear and lock-ups (1.5 by default; 0: no link).',
  lockFlatSpotKm='Wear added to the locking front tyre, in virtual kilometres (1 km by default; 0: no flat spot).',
  strategyEnabled='In the race, the app decides each AI’s stops from its tyre wear, its fuel and the race progress; each AI has its own strategist character. Mandatory stops from the racing rules are still respected.',
  strategyTyreWearPct='Tyre wear from which a careful AI stops (30 % by default: 1 to 1.5 % per lap measured in game); a bold AI goes a little further.',
  strategyFuelMarginLaps='Fuel kept in reserve beyond the finish or the next stop (1 lap by default).',
  strategyUndercut='An AI may stop one lap early to pass a rival just ahead (undercut), or stay out when the rival stops (overcut).',
  strategyRiskPct='Average risk taking of the strategists: longer stints, shorter margins (50 % by default).',
  strategySpreadPct='Character spread between strategists, drawn at random for each AI (60 % by default; 0: all identical).',
  failuresEnabled='Rare progressive failures (small leak, draining battery, overheating): the AI slowly loses pace and returns to the pits for repairs, without an accident. Never on the first lap or at the end of the race.',
  failureRate='Average rate of failures, per race hour and per AI (0.05 by default: about one failure per hour for 20 AI).',
  failureLossPct='Maximum pace loss of a failing AI before its stop (12 % by default).',
  sanTrackLimits='At least three wheels outside the track limits: in practice and qualifying the AI’s lap is invalidated (the next one too if it is in the last sector); in the race, warnings then a penalty.',
  sanTrackWarnings='Warnings given in the race before the track-limits penalty (3 by default; 0: straight penalty).',
  sanCutPenalty='In the race, an AI that gains time by cutting a corner gets a gradual throttle cut, applied on a straight, never in a corner, under flags or with a car right behind.',
  sanCutSeconds='Length of the throttle cut for a cut corner (5 s by default).',
  sanJumpStart='An AI that moves on the grid before the lights go out gets the procedural-offence penalty.',
  sanPitSpeed='An AI that exceeds the pit-lane speed limit (usually 80 km/h) gets the procedural-offence penalty.',
  sanPitExit='An AI that leaves the pit lane before the exit line gets the procedural-offence penalty.',
  sanProcedureKind='Penalty for procedural offences: drive-through (through the pit lane), stop-and-go (stop at its box) or a time penalty added to the result.',
  sanWrongWay='An AI driving the wrong way longer than the tolerated time gets the black flag (disqualified, sent back to its box).',
  sanWrongWaySeconds='Wrong-way time tolerated before the black flag (5 s by default).',
  sanServeLaps='Laps an AI has to serve a drive-through or a stop-and-go; beyond that, black flag (3 by default).',
  sanStopGoSeconds='Length of the stop-and-go at its box (10 s by default).',
  sanTimeSeconds='Time penalty added to the AI’s result (5 s by default).',
  sanUnservedTime='A penalty still unserved at the finish is added to the AI’s result (the app’s corrected classification: AC does not allow its own classification to be changed).',
  sanGiveBack='An AI that passes another outside the track limits must give the place back; otherwise, time penalty.',
  sanGiveBackSeconds='Time left to the AI to give the place back (15 s by default).',
  sanMessages='On-screen notices of AI sanctions (warnings, penalties, black flags); they always stay in the journal.',
  -- Général
  levelPct='Driving level imposed on the AI (AI level): 100% matches level 100 in Content Manager. Above 100%, the effect depends on the level mode in the global options (native if CSP accepts it, otherwise a grip bonus if allowed, otherwise capped at 100%). Each AI then deviates from it according to the level spread, the weather and its focus. Section off: each AI’s native level.',
  aggressionPct='AI aggression: higher means later braking and more determined attacks, with more contact. 100% equals 95% of the maximum native aggression, never more. Each AI deviates from it according to the aggression spread. Section off: native aggression.',
  caution='Base AI caution: safety and braking margin behind another car (1 = native value). Higher: less contact but less attacking. It is then adjusted to the situation (following, yielding, rain, start, incident).',
  brakeZonePct='Emulated braking zone: CSP does not expose the AI braking distance, so the app simulates it through caution when an AI brakes or closes quickly on a car. 100% = native behaviour; above, earlier braking behind a car; below, later.',
  paceSpreadPct='Level difference between drivers: each AI gets a fixed, random offset of at most this percentage above or below the level. 0 = all at the same level; higher = a more spread-out field and more overtaking.',
  aggrSpreadPct='Aggression difference between drivers: each AI gets a fixed, random offset of at most this percentage around the set aggression. Gives more or less attacking drivers.',
  cautSpreadPct='Caution difference between drivers: each AI gets a fixed, random offset of at most this percentage around the set caution. Gives drivers who are more or less careful in traffic.',
  pitExitSpread='Practice and qualifying: at the start of the session, with at least 10 AI in their pit box, each one waits its turn to leave the pits, in a random order, about 10 s apart, instead of all leaving in single file; with many cars the gap shrinks so that all of them are out within 3 min at most (at most a quarter of a short session). An AI moved out of its pit box before its turn leaves at once. 0 = free exit. Applies with the “Staggered pit exits” distribution; on a track day the gap follows the circuit length. Never in the race (grid start).',
  spawnMode='At the start of the session, from the number of AI set below: “On the whole track” places each AI directly on track, over the whole length of the circuit, with varied gaps (about 560 m on average for 45 AI on the 25 km Nordschleife), leaving 300 m free on each side of you and a share of the AI in the pits; “Staggered pit exits” sends them out one after the other (on a track day with a gap proportional to the circuit, all of them out within 10 min at most); “Native” leaves it to the game. Track day: CSP’s AI flood is turned off from the threshold and back on below it; CSP reads this setting at launch, so it takes effect at the next launch. Never in the race (grid start).',
  spawnPitPct='On the whole track: share of the AI, drawn at random, that stay in the pits at the start and leave on their own, as on a real track day (25 % by default: 7 or 8 AI out of 30); the others are placed on track.',
  spawnGapVariation='On the whole track: 0 = AI placed at regular intervals; 100 = gaps drawn at random as in real traffic (a few cars almost nose to tail, others several hundred metres apart), never less than 15 m between two AI. 100 by default.',
  spawnMinCars='Number of AI from which they are spread (10 by default); below it the game’s own start is kept and, on a track day, CSP’s AI flood keeps the AI around you.',
  floodRangeKm='Track day, below the threshold: distance around you over which CSP’s AI flood spawns and removes the AI (1 km = CSP setting). Beyond 2 km (the limit of CSP’s settings page), the effect is not guaranteed. Takes effect at the next launch.',
  -- Cession
  yieldEnabled='Standard yielding: a slower AI moves aside to let a clearly faster car behind (player or AI) through, as on a track day, in practice or in qualifying. No effect in the Race, where only a blue flag, a faster class or an accepted headlight flash makes a car yield.',
  yieldSide='Side the AI moves to when yielding: left, right or automatic (from the position of the car behind, then the free side). It is also the reference for the “Opposite to yield side” overtaking side, the line bias of slow drivers and the way round an obstacle when both sides are equal.',
  yieldOffset='Lateral offset of the yielding AI, as a fraction of the track half-width (0.65 = about two thirds of the way to the edge). Reduced to as little as 40% in corners and restored on the exit, increased slightly after an insistent headlight flash, always limited by the maximum offset and the real track width.',
  yieldDetectBehind='Minimum distance at which the AI watches for a faster car behind. The real range grows with the speed difference (difference times the rear horizon, up to 400 m) and by 30% on a wet track if the Weather section is on.',
  yieldBehindMargin='Minimum speed advantage the car behind needs for an AI to let it through (standard yielding). Adjusted to each driver’s courtesy and reduced at short range. Lower: the AI yield more often, even to cars that are barely faster.',
  yieldHorizon='Closing time: the AI yields if the car behind will catch it within this time. Longer: earlier yielding; shorter: last-moment yielding. Lengthened by half when a headlight flash is accepted.',
  confirmTime='How long a situation must hold before the AI decides to yield or overtake: filters out decisions on a momentary gap. Shorter: sharper reactions; longer: fewer pointless manoeuvres.',
  yieldHoldMin='Minimum time spent aside once the yield has begun, even if the car behind passes quickly: avoids moving back and forth.',
  yieldHoldAfterMin='After the car behind has passed, the AI stays aside for a moment, drawn between this minimum and the maximum then adjusted to the driver’s patience, before returning to its line: the passing car can move back across without being impeded.',
  yieldHoldAfterMax='Upper bound of the hold after the pass (see the minimum). Under a blue flag or ahead of a faster class, a yield to several cars adds 1 s to let the whole train through.',
  yieldSlowdownPct='Level reduction while yielding, to make the pass easier. Yields under a blue flag or to a faster class use a fixed 8% reduction. Also used when a blocked AI with no free side is holding up a car right behind it.',
  inviteSlowdownPct='Extra reduction to invite the car behind to pass: if it stays within 25 m for more than 12 s while the AI is yielding (or sooner after an insistent flash), the AI gradually slows by this percentage.',
  qualiTraffic='Practice and qualifying, as in F1: a car that is not on a flying lap (out-lap from the pits to the line, tyres to warm up; cool-down or aborted lap, clearly slower, until the line; in-lap; damaged car) lets the flying car coming up behind within 3 s go by, as under a blue flag, even with a car ahead of it; it never attacks it; an AI leaving the pits waits at the end of the pit lane until a flying car has gone by. You too: on an out-lap or cool-down lap, a message warns you that a flying AI is coming ("Flying lap"). An AI on a flying lap does not yield to a car that is not. Never in the race or on a track day.',
  yieldMaxHold='Maximum duration of a yield. Beyond it, the AI returns to its line and ignores that car for 10 s: a car that does not pass cannot hold it up forever.',
  pressureEnabled='Reaction to pressure: a car sitting right behind at a similar speed is eventually let through once the pressure duration has elapsed. Outside the Race only, like standard yielding.',
  pressureGap='Distance under which a car behind at a similar speed (8 km/h difference at most) counts as sitting right on the gearbox.',
  pressureTime='Continuous pressure time after which the AI lets the car through. Shorter: the AI give way as soon as you sit right behind them; longer: they resist more.',
  -- Drapeaux bleus
  blueFlagEnabled='Blue flag: an AI about to be lapped moves aside to let the lapping car (player or AI) through, with a limited loss of pace. Never on a Track day.',
  blueFlagRange='Blue flag range behind the AI. Multiplied by 1.5 for a backmarker two or more laps down, and for a faster class.',
  blueFlagSide='Side the AI moves to under a blue flag, and in a race ahead of a faster class (outside the race, it is the yield side): automatic (from the position of the lapping car, then the free side), left or right.',
  -- Appels de phares
  flashMode='Player headlight flashes taken into account: never, lapped cars only, or always. Outside the Race, an accepted flash makes the AI yield; in the Race, “Lapped cars” only targets the AI you are lapping, and “Always” counts as a request to pass for all of them.',
  flashRange='Maximum distance between the flashing car and the targeted AI for the flash to be seen (player and AI alike).',
  flashMinAdvantage='Minimum speed advantage for a headlight flash to be accepted: below it, the AI judges you are not faster and holds its line. A held-up AI only flashes the car ahead if its usual pace beats that car by at least this amount. Beyond 60 m you must also be able to reach the AI before its yield ends (maximum yield duration minus 5 s): flashed too early, it would move over for nothing.',
  flashCloseAdvantage='Speed advantage that is enough within 60 m (30 m up to Beta 1.28). Negative: even slightly slower, your flash is understood; −5 km/h by default outside the race: stuck behind a car at the same speed, the flash means “let me by”.',
  flashRequestTTL='How long a request to pass (your flash or an AI’s) stays valid; extended if the AI is in a corner when it is flashed.',
  flashObliviousPct='Probability that an AI misses a first headlight flash. A second flash within 4 s is always seen and makes the request urgent.',
  flashMaxReactors='Number of AI ahead of you that react to the same flash, from the nearest to the furthest, 0.5 s apart: useful to get past a short train of cars.',
  hornEnabled='The player’s horn also counts as a headlight flash (handy if the headlight flash is not calibrated).',
  aiFlashEnabled='AI-to-AI headlight flashes: an AI held up behind a slower car gives it a visible flash, and that car yields as it would to a player flash. In the Race, only to lap a car or for a faster class (an LMP1 behind an LMP3, for example); never between cars fighting for position.',
  -- Dépassement et défense
  overtakeEnabled='Overtakes driven by the app: the AI prepares, pulls out, runs side by side, then moves back across with a margin, always respecting an occupied side. Off: the AI only overtake in their native way.',
  overtakeSide='Overtaking side: opposite to the yield side (consistent on a track day), automatic (in the Race, from the position of the car ahead and the next corner; elsewhere, opposite to the yield side), left or right. The free side always has priority.',
  overtakeOffset='Lateral offset while overtaking, as a fraction of the track half-width; reduced to as little as 40% in corners and restored on the exit.',
  overtakeDetectAhead='Distance ahead at which a slower car is considered for an overtake, and for chaining a second one.',
  overtakeAheadMargin='Minimum speed advantage to start an overtake, adjusted to the driver’s confidence and the distance; halved against a slower class.',
  overtakeHorizon='Closing time: the AI starts the overtake if it will catch the car ahead within this time.',
  overtakeClearMargin='Lead to build over the overtaken car, on top of the two car lengths, before moving back across.',
  overtakeRestTime='Pause after an overtake before trying another, to settle on the line. 0 = the AI can attack again straight away.',
  overtakeChain='Chain overtakes: if another slower car is just ahead and the lane stays clear, the AI passes it in the same move without returning to its line.',
  stuckTime='Time spent held up behind a car, below its usual pace, before trying an overtake even without a large speed difference; adjusted to the driver’s patience. Also used for AI-to-AI headlight flashes.',
  stuckPaceRatio='Held-up pace threshold: the AI considers itself held up below this fraction of its own usual pace at that point of the track, learned lap after lap.',
  pullOutGapBase='In the Race, distance at which the AI pulls out to attack: this base plus the gain multiplied by the speed (m/s), at most 22 m.',
  pullOutGapPerMps='Pull-out distance added per metre per second of speed: at 180 km/h (50 m/s), 0.25 adds 12.5 m to the base.',
  slipstreamEnabled='Slipstream: on a fast straight (over 150 km/h) behind a car, the AI lowers its caution after 1 s to make the most of the tow.',
  defenseEnabled='Defending, in the Race only: against a close attacker, the AI may cover its line once per attacker and per lap, with a probability tied to its confidence. Never against a car lapping it or from a faster class, never weaving and never under braking.',
  defenseMaxOffset='Maximum lateral offset of a defence, as a fraction of the track half-width: a single move that leaves room for the attacker.',
  defenseCooldown='Minimum pause between two defences by the same AI, whoever the attacker: it does not move at every attempt.',
  defenseTriggerGap='Distance to the attacker below which a defence can be triggered.',
  -- Trajectoires
  rampSpeed='Maximum speed of the lateral movements driven by the app, in half-widths per second, adjusted for speed. Higher: sharper manoeuvres; lower: smoother ones.',
  rampAccel='How fast lateral movements build up: higher, the AI reaches its movement speed sooner.',
  lowPassTau='Smoothing of the lateral target: longer gives smoother lines but later reactions.',
  maxSideOffset='Maximum lateral offset allowed for all manoeuvres (yielding, overtaking, defending, avoidance), as a fraction of the half-width; always limited by the real track width.',
  cornerMin='Straight threshold: corner factor above which the track is considered straight enough to start a yield or an overtake, flash the headlights or apply spacing. Higher: manoeuvres kept for real straights.',
  cornerYawRef='Yaw rate (rad/s) from which the car is considered in the middle of a corner; used to compute the corner factor (1 = straight, 0 = tight corner). Lower: more of the track classed as corners.',
  lateralCooldown='Minimum delay between two changes of targeted side (left, line, right) during a manoeuvre. Following an offset that varies on the same side, for example reduced in a corner, is not one (since 1.3.7). Beyond six changes per minute, the AI goes 20 s without manoeuvres (anti-weaving); avoidance and recovery are never subject to it.',
  emergencyReleaseFactor='If a car occupies the side the AI was moving towards, the AI returns to its line at this multiple of the speed: 2 = twice as fast as a normal movement.',
  -- Personnalité
  reactMin='Reaction time of each driver, drawn between this minimum and the maximum: delay between the decision to yield and the start of the movement.',
  reactMax='Upper bound of the reaction time (see the minimum). Higher: drivers slower to react.',
  awMin='Awareness: random delay, drawn per driver between this minimum and the maximum, added to the reaction time before a car behind is noticed.',
  awMax='Upper bound of the awareness delay (see the minimum). Higher: some drivers notice a faster car behind late.',
  paceDriftPct='Slow drift in each driver’s pace, over cycles of 1.5 to 4 minutes: amplitude as a percentage of the level.',
  personalLineJitter='Slow variation of the line, specific to each driver, when no car is within 40 m, as a fraction of the half-width: the AI do not all drive exactly the same line. Never counted by the anti-weaving limit.',
  lapseEnabled='Brief lapses in focus: now and then a driver loses a few per cent of pace for one to three seconds, never side by side, while defending or at the start of a yield.',
  lapseIntervalMin='Interval between two lapses in focus of a driver, drawn between this minimum and the maximum; shortened by fatigue in endurance.',
  lapseIntervalMax='Upper bound of the interval between two lapses in focus. Higher: drivers more consistent over time.',
  lapseDurMin='Duration of a lapse in focus, drawn between this minimum and the maximum.',
  lapseDurMax='Upper bound of the duration of a lapse in focus. Higher: longer dips.',
  lapseDropMin='Pace lost during a lapse in focus, drawn between this minimum and the maximum.',
  lapseDropMax='Upper bound of the pace lost during a lapse in focus.',
  laneBiasEnabled='Pace-based line: the slowest drivers naturally run slightly over on the yield side, as on a track day; the fastest keep the racing line.',
  laneBiasMax='Line offset of the slowest driver, as a fraction of the half-width; together with the line variation, the total stays limited to 0.25.',
  mistakesEnabled='AI driving mistakes, triggered through native CSP functions: a wheel lock-up under braking or wheelspin out of a corner, which may end in an off, a spin or a crash (then a yellow flag, a neutralisation or the Safety Car). More frequent for a weaker driver, a tired one (Endurance layer), one in a lapse of focus, or in the rain. Never under flags or in the three minutes after a neutralisation, at the start, on pit exit, side by side, within 40 m of you, or more than once every three minutes for the same AI. Written to the log.',
  mistakeRate='Base mistake rate, per hour and per AI, before the level, fatigue, focus and rain factors: 0.5 by default (a grid of 20 AI: about ten mistakes an hour, not all of which end in a crash). 0 = none.',
  -- Départ
  startDelay='Race only: for these seconds after the start, the app commands no lateral movement, no yielding, no overtaking and no avoidance; the AI follow their native line.',
  startLaneHold='Race only: each AI records its position on the grid and keeps it after the green light while a car is running alongside it (and while braking), instead of heading straight for the racing line and cutting across its neighbours; it then joins the line gradually, never towards an occupied side. The log notes the recorded offset and the offset measured on return.',
  firstLapCare='Race only: at the start and at every restart (green flag after the safety car, end of the restart lap, standing restart), each AI drives its first lap as a careful driver: the gap to the car ahead is counted in time (same effect in F1, GT or road cars), every braking zone of the lap is anticipated (braking points learnt from the leading cars, braking of the car ahead), the radar lifts off before it brakes, no defence, no tow-breaking and no dirty air, overtakes only on a real straight with a real advantage, avoidance only of a stopped or very slow car, aggression slightly lower. For each AI the mode ends at its first crossing of the line, when the pack has spread around it or after 3 minutes, then it is released over 3 seconds. The log notes the start and the end.',
  startCautionMult='Race only: caution multiplied at the start of the race, then brought gradually back to normal during the settling time. A little higher = fewer clashes at the first corner, but too high and the middle of the pack lifts off before the corner.',
  startAggrMult='Race only: aggression multiplied at the start of the race (below 1 = calmer), then brought gradually back to normal during the settling time.',
  startRelax='Race only: length of the settling time after the start delay. Caution and aggression return to normal, and no overtaking or yielding is commanded during this phase.',
  -- Accidents
  obstacleEnabled='Avoidance: an AI approaching a car that is stopped, very slow, spinning or crashed on track slows down and goes round it on the free side; with no clear lane, it slows hard behind it. Never subject to the anti-weaving limit.',
  obstacleSpeedKmh='Speed under which a car slow for 2 s becomes an obstacle, avoided only if the AI arrives on it at least 15 km/h faster or if it is an incident (in a traffic jam, the AI follows). Also used to recognise that a crashed AI has almost stopped and that a slow incident is still going on.',
  obstacleDetect='Distance ahead at which a stopped or crashed car is taken into account: further, the AI slows down and picks its side earlier.',
  obstacleLevelDrop='Level reduction while avoiding, to get round the obstacle safely; with no clear lane, the AI slows down even more.',
  obstacleCautionMult='Caution multiplied while avoiding: more margin from the other cars going round the same obstacle.',
  avoidOffset='Lateral offset to go round an obstacle, as a fraction of the half-width; limited by the maximum offset.',
  contactRecovery='Recovery after contact: after a hit (a sharp loss of speed close to a car), the AI drives more calmly for 4 s; almost stopped, it waits before moving off.',
  incidentStop='Crashed AI stop: an AI that has just crashed (contact, off or spin, then almost stopped) stays still for a few seconds, like a driver collecting their thoughts, then moves off when the track behind is clear. Off track only: on the track itself the AI moves off at once, never left stationary on the racing line.',
  incidentStopMin='Length of a crashed AI’s stop, drawn between this minimum and the maximum; at most 10 s of extra waiting if the track is not clear.',
  incidentStopMax='Upper bound of a crashed AI’s stop; the actual length is drawn at random for each accident.',
  rejoinGap='Clear distance required behind a crashed AI before it moves off: no car closer on the track.',
  yellowEnabled='Yellow flag: around an incident (a car stopped, spinning or going the wrong way, player included), a zone where cars slow down and do not overtake. Incident on track: double waved yellow, zone delta time; off track: single yellow, slightly reduced pace, no delta. The AI brake progressively on the approach.',
  yellowSpeedKmh='Fallback speed under double yellow (incident on track), only where no pace is known yet (first lap); elsewhere, the zone delta time applies. No effect when a Slow Zone is set (Endurance).',
  zoneDeltaPct='Double waved yellow (incident on track): in the zone, the reference time is this percentage slower than the usual pace (15%: about 0.87 times the speed, an app value; the FIA publishes no threshold, it requires drivers to slow down significantly, not to overtake and to give up any significant lap time; a Slow Zone, when set, replaces it). The delta is reset at the zone entry, allows braking from your speed at the approach board, and is judged at each mini-sector and at the zone exit. The AI follow the same reference with a small margin.',
  slowZoneKmh='Endurance Slow Zone (Le Mans, WEC): in a double yellow zone, the reference time is set at this fixed speed instead of the usual pace; 0 = zone delta time. The Endurance rules set it to 80 km/h. The delta is judged on each mini-sector, as in F1: going faster then slower evens out. The AI are limited to this speed in the zone.',
  yellowBefore='Start of the yellow zone before the incident: the AI already follow the zone reference time there and stop overtaking. Longer on a fast circuit, to brake in time.',
  yellowAfter='End of the yellow zone after the incident: beyond it, the AI accelerate again and may overtake again.',
  yellowHold='How long the zone stays active after the incident is over.',
  yellowDecel='Approach deceleration: the AI brake at this rate down to the reference at the zone start, and the delta time allows braking at this deceleration from your speed at the signal (after a 2.5 s reaction). Higher: later, harder braking. Overtaking is forbidden from 150 m before the zone, for the AI and the player alike.',
  drsBeepVolume='Volume of the beeps (DRS available and delta time), the same in the cockpit and exterior views, on top of the game’s master volume. 200% by default; above 100%, the sound may clip slightly.',
  rulesDrs='DRS rules applied to every car, player included, as race control does in F1: disabled in the rain, under flags (neutralisation, red flag, the car’s yellow zone) and, in the race, for the leader’s first laps after the start, after a Safety Car and after a red flag (never after a VSC or an FCY: F1 Sporting Regulations, article 22.1). Practice and qualifying: rain and flags only. Never on a track day. GT3 and WEC cars have no DRS: these rules apply to cars that have one.',
  rulesDrsLaps='Laps the leader must complete after the start, then after a Safety Car or a red flag, before DRS is enabled. They are measured by the app on track (the game’s counter counts crossing the line just after the start); app loaded mid-race: no wait. F1 since 2024: 1 lap (2 before); F1 2026 rules: 0 (active aero with no wait); DTM (2013 to 2020) did not allow it in the opening laps.',
  aiDrs='AI whose car has DRS open it in the zones, as you do: when CSP reports it available (DRS zone and the track’s detection gap; when disabled by the racing rules, it never is), on a straight and not braking; it closes under braking. The log summary counts the AI openings every minute, including those requested by the app.',
  drsAssist='For your DRS-equipped car: a beep when DRS becomes available, and the flag panel (if enabled, Accidents section) shows a “DRS” badge: white outline when available, solid white light when open. Availability is AC’s own (the track’s DRS zones, within one second at the detection point); the app does not open it for you, and the racing rules may disable it. Under flags DRS is not allowed: the flag stays displayed with the line “DRS disabled under flags”, without a beep.',
  penaltyDisqualify='Number of offences under flags allowed in the session (negative delta time, overtaking, restarting outside the pits or a missed mandatory stop for you, overtaking for an AI): up to that number, a throttle cut for you and a few seconds off the throttle for an AI; beyond it, disqualification: AC’s black flag for you, and for an AI a return to its pit box, parked until the end of the session. The panel shows your count.',
  aiPenalties='Sanctions for AI that overtake under yellow, under neutralisation or under a red flag (a crashed, stopped or very slow car, or a car in the pits or off track, may be passed): a few seconds off the throttle, then disqualification beyond the number of offences allowed. The car passed illegally may retake its place without sanction. Practice, qualifying and race, never on a track day.',
  playerPenalties='Player penalties, in practice, qualifying and race (never on a track day). Delta time: under VSC, FCY, Safety Car, red flag and double waved yellow, crossing a mini-sector (about every 250 m) with a negative delta beyond the tolerance. Overtaking: passing a car in a yellow zone or in the 150 m before it, under neutralisation or a red flag, except a car that has crashed or only just moved off again, is stopped, very slow, in the pits, off track or serving a sanction; an overtake made within 10 s of the flag (while braking) is forgiven if the position is given back, even after the flag has ended, and retaking a place an AI gives back to you is not an overtake; a car passed illegally may retake its place without sanction while the flag lasts. After a Safety Car, no overtaking until the line. AC adds no time to the classification: the closest native penalty is applied, a throttle cut, announced on screen and on the panel, and written to the log.',
  deltaTolerance='Margin below zero allowed when crossing a mini-sector before a delta time penalty (0.50 s by default). Under neutralisation and red flag, the delta starts at +1 s; it always allows braking at the approach deceleration from your speed at the signal.',
  deltaBeeps='Delta time beeps, as in an F1 car: closer and closer together as the delta nears zero, very fast when it is negative. Volume: that of the beeps (Racing rules tab).',
  penaltySeconds='Length of the throttle cut imposed by a player penalty, and how long a sanctioned AI lifts off. The throttle cut only works with race settings that allow it.',
  yellowHud='Also forces AC’s small native yellow flag on your HUD when approaching a yellow zone (150 to 800 m before, depending on your speed) and under neutralisation, then hands control back to AC. Off by default since 1.5.0: the AdvancedIA panel replaces it.',
  flagPanel='AdvancedIA flag panel, readable at a glance while driving (in-game overlay, adjustable in SETTINGS › Overlay: unlock it to move and resize it). Coloured band and flag with their code (SC, VSC, FCY, ! single yellow, !! double yellow, yellow and red stripes for a slippery surface), large instruction and large delta time (green when positive, flashing red when negative); pills for a penalty, a position to give back, PIT under a red flag, the gap of the car lapping you under a blue flag, “No overtaking” and “DRS disabled”; app version at the top. Red and blue flash, and so does a new flag for a few seconds. The game’s own flags and penalties (black, drive-through with the laps left, chequered, last lap, blue, yellow) are always shown there, even with the panel off or the app stopped: hide the original ones in CSP (“Hide race flags”).',
  yellowMessage='On-screen messages: yellow flag, neutralisation (FCY, VSC, Safety Car), red flag, green flag, penalties and disqualifications.',
  -- Règles de course
  noMoveBraking='Common rule: no change of direction under braking. No lateral manoeuvre starts or continues under braking (pedal beyond 20%), except avoidance and recovery.',
  neutralization='Response to a major incident, in the Race only: local yellow zone only, FCY, VSC or Safety Car (if a car is chosen and on the grid, otherwise FCY). Delta time as in F1: a reference time slower than the usual pace (by default FCY +50%, VSC +30%; GT and Endurance rules: FCY +150%, the equivalent of the regulation 80 km/h; Safety Car: FCY delta until the queue), shown on the panel and judged at each mini-sector; the AI follow the same reference. VSC ending announced a few seconds before the green flag; after the Safety Car, no overtaking until the line. With “Safety Car if the incident escalates”, a VSC or FCY can give way to the Safety Car.',
  majorIncidentTime='A car stopped on track for longer than this is a major incident and triggers the chosen neutralisation (Race only). It is only taken back to its pit box afterwards (this delay plus 5 s), leaving race control time to decide.',
  fcySpeedKmh='Fallback speed under FCY, only where no pace is known yet (first lap); elsewhere, the FCY delta time applies. Also while the Safety Car is being called.',
  fcyDeltaPct='Under FCY (and while the Safety Car is being called), the reference time is this percentage slower than the usual pace at each point (50%: about 0.67 times the speed), for you and the AI alike; stricter than the VSC. GT and Endurance rules: 150% (about 0.4 times the speed), the equivalent of the fixed 80 km/h of the WEC and the GT World Challenge on most circuits.',
  vscDeltaPct='Under VSC, the reference time is this percentage slower than the usual pace at each point (30%: about 0.77 times the speed, an app value; the FIA imposes a minimum time computed by its own unit, which is not published), for you and the AI alike.',
  neutralizationHold='Minimum length of a neutralisation (FCY, VSC) after the last major incident, before the green flag. A Safety Car also stays out for at least 60 s.',
  safetyCarSpeedKmh='Speed of the Safety Car on track; the AI catch it up and then run in single file behind it.',
  restartOvertake='After a restart behind the Safety Car (red flag or an ordinary deployment): overtaking allowed at the control line (usual rule), or at the green flag, as soon as it pits. A standing start always allows overtaking from lights out.',
  safetyCarEscalation='Safety Car escalation, in the Race: the set neutralisation (VSC, FCY or local yellow zone) responds first to the major incident; if the incident escalates, the Safety Car is deployed, as race control decides: at least 2 cars stopped on track at the same time, or a car stopped for 30 s longer than the major incident delay (long recovery). It is called first (field on the FCY delta), then leaves the pits; after a VSC, your delta restarts at +1 s. On by default in the race, with every rule set. The chosen model (by default the Ferrari 458 Italia Stage 3 from the base game) must be on the grid; otherwise the VSC or FCY carries on. The red flag still takes priority.',
  redFlagEnabled='Red flag, in practice, qualifying and race: when several cars are stopped on track at the same time (stationary for 5 s; your car is not counted), the race (or the session) is suspended. All AI slow down without overtaking, return to their pit box and stay there; you must also enter the pit lane: the restart waits for you (at most until the maximum delay) and restarting outside the pits is penalised; an AI stopped on track is recovered to its pit box. In a race, a real restart: AI released from the pits in classification order, a restart lap behind the Safety Car (without it: under VSC or FCY), no overtaking until the line. AC’s race clock keeps running.',
  redFlagCars='Number of cars stopped for 10 s because of an incident (damage, spin, stopped off the racing line or off the track) that triggers the red flag, only when they really block the track (no 2.5 m passage); undamaged cars waiting in the queue behind the accident do not count. Lower: more frequent red flags; 3 corresponds to a pile-up.',
  redFlagSpeedKmh='Fallback speed under a red flag, only where no pace is known yet; elsewhere, the red flag delta time applies until the pit lane. No overtaking. It also sets the maximum length of the suspension (one and a quarter laps at this speed).',
  redDeltaPct='Red flag: reference time this percentage slower until the pit lane (60%: about 0.63 times the speed), for you and the AI alike.',
  redFlagHold='Minimum length of the suspension. The green flag is given once all the AI are in, the track is clear and this time has passed; at the latest after one and a quarter laps at the return speed, plus the pit lane (5 to 30 minutes depending on the circuit).',
  redRestart='How a race restarts after a red flag (Race only). Rolling start (RS): one relaunch lap behind the Safety Car (or under VSC, FCY), then the race restarts rolling. Standing start (SS, F1): after that lap, each car drives to its grid slot in race order, slots closed up; five lights, then the start at lights out. Lights in the overlay only. Without a reliable grid or the CSP functions it needs: rolling start, with the reason in the log.',
  redFlagWork='Red flag: AI back in their pit box change tyres, and the stop counts towards the mandatory stops (F1: tyre changes allowed under a red flag). Off (WEC, GT): no work on the cars under a red flag.',
  mandatoryStops='Pit stop required in the race, within the set window (1 = one mandatory stop, 0 = none); after that, everyone makes as many stops as their strategy needs. A stop made with the pit lane closed does not count; F1 rules: no longer mandatory if the race has turned wet. You too: a reminder on the panel when the window opens and towards its end, and a penalty if the stop is missing when it closes (a window that closes at 100% never closes). If the session declares its own window (CSP), that one is used. The stops are spread among the AI, at most three called in at once.',
  pitWindowOpenPct='Pit window opening, as a percentage of the race (leader laps, or time for a timed race). GT World Challenge Sprint: 42%, i.e. 25 minutes into one hour. F1 imposes no window: its rules open it at 20%.',
  pitWindowClosePct='Pit window closing, as a percentage of the race: AI that have not yet stopped are called in before this limit. GT Sprint: 58% (35 minutes); F1: 95% (stop made before the end); 100%: never closed.',
  pitTyres='New tyres at every visit to the pit box in the race, as soon as a pit rule is active (mandatory stops, maximum stint or minimum stop time), taking the weather into account (rain or dry).',
  pitRepair='Repairs in the race: every time a damaged AI visits its pit box (a rules stop or a native stop, for fuel for instance), bodywork, suspension and engine are repaired; it stays there for the repair time, 3 s plus according to the damage, 60 s at most. On by default with every rule set (championships allow repairs in the pits).',
  pitCompoundChange='Formula 1: at the stop, the AI fits a different dry compound from the one it started on (two compounds mandatory in a dry race; no longer mandatory if the race turns wet).',
  minPitTime='Minimum stationary time in the pit box; 0 = free. No minimum time in GT Sprint or in F1: useful to mimic refuelling or a driver change.',
  pitFuel='AI fuel at enforced stops (mandatory stops, maximum stint, tyre change for the weather, red flag), in the race. Refuel at the stop: the AI takes just what it needs until the next planned stop or the finish, with a reserve (endurance, base rules). Race fuel from the start: the AI starts with fuel for the whole race (within the tank size) and no longer refuels, unless that is not enough to finish (F1 and GT Sprint, where refuelling is banned). Left to AC: the app does not touch the fuel. Consumption is measured lap by lap (otherwise AC’s estimate is used); while it is unknown, nothing is imposed. No effect without fuel consumption in the session.',
  maxStintMin='Maximum stint: beyond it, the AI is called into the pits, except in the last 5 per cent of the race; 0 = free. The Endurance value of 50 min is an approximation.',
  pitsClosed='Pit entry closed during neutralisations: under FCY (WEC), and during the first three Safety Car laps (WEC). In the GT World Challenge, the pits stay open under FCY. The AI are not called in, and a stop made with the pit lane closed does not count towards the mandatory stops; a tyre change for rain is still allowed (emergency stop). The panel shows “PIT LANE CLOSED”.',
  pitMessage='On-screen message when the mandatory pit window opens, with its limits.',
  overtakeMode='Formula 1 2026: an AI within the detection gap of the car ahead at the detection point (end of the lap) gains extra level on the straights of the following lap (Overtake Mode). Disabled in the rain, under flags, and after the start or a restart until the leader has reached the detection point. Race only, AI only (AC does not give this boost to the player).',
  overtakeModeGap='Gap to the car ahead, in seconds, measured at the detection point at the end of the lap, to get Overtake Mode (1 s in F1 2026).',
  overtakeModeBoostPct='Level gain on the straights of the lap following the detection; none in corners.',
  bopEnabled='Emulated BoP: within each class, the AI level slowly converges towards the class median pace, with a bounded correction. Car weight and power are never changed.',
  bopStrengthPct='Maximum level correction the BoP applies to an AI, up or down, updated every minute.',
  proAmEnabled='Pro-Am ratings: each AI gets a rating that stays the same all weekend (Platinum, Gold, Silver or Bronze); lower ratings are slightly slower (up to 4%) and more careful.',
  predictableSlowClass='Endurance: an AI of a slower class caught by a faster class holds its line instead of moving aside; it is up to the faster car to overtake cleanly.',
  -- Classes et endurance
  enduranceOverlay='Endurance layer (Track day and Race): pace that varies from one stint to the next, a slight drop over a stint, extra caution for 8 s on pit exit, and more frequent lapses in focus after 1 h 30 without an incident.',
  multiClass='Multi-class: the AI are split into classes by pace (or by the class patterns); a slower class yields to a faster one, which overtakes it more easily. Without a large enough pace gap, the grid stays a single class; an AI still learning its pace is compared with no class.',
  classGapPct='Pace gap between two consecutive cars, ranked from fastest to slowest, that opens a new class; four classes at most.',
  classYieldMargin='Minimum speed advantage of a faster class for the AI to let it through.',
  stintLevelSwing='With the Endurance layer (track day and race): level variation from one stint to the next, drawn for each AI at each pit stop.',
  stintDegradationPct='With the Endurance layer (track day and race): level drop over a stint, reached after 40 minutes, reset in the pits.',
  -- Météo
  weatherAdaptation='On a wet track, the AI lower their level (by up to 10%) and their aggression, increase their caution and watch further behind them.',
  weatherTyresEnabled='Tyres: in the rain, the AI fit wet or intermediate tyres at their stop, and go back to slicks when the track dries; the change is confirmed after 15 s of stable weather.',
  weatherMessage='On-screen message when the track turns wet or dries out again, announcing the pace and tyre adaptation.',
  rainGripBoostPct='Cars without wet tyres: a small grip bonus proportional to wetness, so they stay drivable in the rain (0 = none). Requires the “Manage dry and wet tyres” option.',
  -- Panneaux lumineux (Beta 1.37)
  lightsYellow='Yellow on the panels for a yellow zone ahead of you, a double yellow, the Safety Car, VSC and FCY. Always on: it is the basic signal of the panels.',
  lightsRed='Red on the panels under a red flag. Off: yellow instead. Red is only written if AC’s flags are hidden in CSP (Content Manager › CSP › GUI): the same value is the black flag in AC’s own display.',
  lightsBlue='Blue on the panels when a faster car is lapping you. Off: the next colour shows (green, or the flag handed back to AC).',
  lightsWhite='White on the panels for a very slow car ahead of you (below half its usual pace). Off: the next colour shows; the overlay SLOW VEHICLE flag stays.',
  lightsOrange='Orange on the panels when the track turns slippery (rain starting). Off: the next colour shows.',
  lightsGreen='Green on the panels at the end of a yellow zone, of a neutralisation and at a restart. Off: the flag goes back to AC when the incident ends.',
  lightsGreenTime='Green duration after a neutralisation or a restart (5 s by default); the end of a yellow zone keeps three fifths of it (3 s by default). 0: no green.',
  lightsHold='Delay before switching to a less important colour or handing the flag back to AC, so the panels never flicker. A more important colour always shows at once.',
  -- Espacement
  trafficGapSeconds='Track day only: base gap aimed for between two AI, in seconds at their speed, plus 15 m.',
  trafficDensityGain='Track day only: distance added to the target gap for each AI present; the more cars there are, the more they spread out, within the length of the circuit.',
  trafficVariation='Track day only: random variation of the target gap from one AI to another, to avoid traffic that is too regular.',
  trafficMaxReduction='Track day only: maximum speed reduction, applied in 6 s pulses every 18 s, to an AI too close to the one ahead.',
 },
 globals={
  overlayLocked='Locked, the overlay stays in place and lets clicks through. Unlocked, it stays visible: drag it to move it, pull its bottom right corner for its size, right-click to lock it.',
  overlayScale='Size of the in-game overlay (1.00x: 600 px wide). Also set by pulling its bottom right corner once unlocked.',
  overlayBackground='Overlay background: opaque (at the opacity set), dimmed (lighter) or transparent (text and flags only), as in SuperHUD.',
  overlayContent='Opacity of the text, flags and pills of the overlay, separate from the background opacity.',
  overlaySession='Top line of the overlay: position, lap of the total (or time left), best lap, mandatory stop and fuel left in laps. It replaces the AC session box.',
  overlayExplain='Under the instruction, an explanation of what is happening: yellow flag, double yellow, Safety Car, VSC, FCY, red flag, blue flag.',
  overlayRecap='At the end of a race: chequered flag, congratulations for your place, places gained or lost since the start, best lap compared with the whole field, overtakes and laps.',
  overlayNativeBox='Hides the original AC session box at the top of the screen (lap 2/8, time left, RACE OVER) while the overlay session line replaces it. Restored when the app unloads.',
  overlayMessages='Shows the Assetto Corsa messages (assists, warnings, invalidated lap) on the top line of the overlay, for as long as they last.',
  overlayBlockMessages='Hides the original display of the Assetto Corsa messages shown on the overlay (on by default). At launch the app checks that hidden messages still reach the overlay; otherwise hiding lifts itself until the next launch (log).',
  cspHideMessages='Ticks the CSP option “Hide system messages” (cfg/extension/gui.ini) while the app is active and the overlay takes over the messages: AC’s top banner (aids…) disappears. AC’s red cut and penalty banners (CUT DETECTED, PENALTY…) stay drawn by AC: CSP cannot hide them; the overlay does not show them again (no duplicate). The option stays ticked while the app is active, also with the game closed (Content Manager shows it ticked); your original value is kept and given back only when you switch this off, switch the app off (APP button) or turn off the overlay’s message takeover; neither panic nor closing the game gives it back. Unticked in Content Manager while the app is active, it is ticked again at the next launch: switch it off here instead. An option you already ticked is never touched; after uninstalling the app, untick it in Content Manager › Custom Shaders Patch › GUI if you wish. CSP may only apply it at the next game launch. If messages no longer reach the overlay, the option is restored by itself (log).',
  cspHideFlags='Ticks the CSP option “Hide race flags” (cfg/extension/gui.ini) while the app is active and its flag panel (“App flag panel while driving”) stays ticked in the profile in use: AC’s own flags (top left corner) disappear, the overlay shows them, and red on the track light panels becomes possible. The option stays ticked while the app is active, also with the game closed (Content Manager shows it ticked); your original value is kept and given back only when you switch this off, switch the app off (APP button) or turn off the app’s flag panel; neither panic nor closing the game gives it back. Unticked in Content Manager while the app is active, it is ticked again at the next launch: switch it off here instead. An option you already ticked is never touched; after uninstalling the app, untick it in Content Manager › Custom Shaders Patch › GUI if you wish. CSP may only apply it at the next game launch.',
  overlayClickThrough='Locked, the overlay lets mouse clicks through (as in SuperHUD). Unticked: right-click the overlay to unlock it.',
  overlayHideReplay='Hides the overlay during replays.',
  overlayHidePits='Hides the overlay while your car is in the pit lane.',
  levelMode='How a level above 100% is applied: automatic (native if CSP accepts it, otherwise a grip bonus if allowed, otherwise the cap), native if confirmed, grip bonus allowed, or capped at 100%.',
  useGripBoost='Allows the grip bonus to emulate a level above 100% when CSP does not accept it natively. Changes the grip of the AI cars: only switch it on knowingly.',
  useBrakeAssist='Front radar, on by default: an AI about to hit the car ahead (player or AI) first lifts off, then, with contact imminent, adds light, progressive braking, like a driver anticipating. Never a stab of the brakes nor a sideways move; the brake only adds to the AI’s own, and the throttle and steering stay its own. Active from the starting grid, never in the pits.',
  overrideAwareness='When the app imposes a lateral offset, the AI ignore their native avoidance and strictly follow the target. More precise, but removes a native safeguard: off by default.',
  panelOpacity='Opacity of the flag overlay background: 100% opaque, 0% fully transparent (only the flag, the texts and the delta remain). Shared by all sessions.',
  panelShow='Only with information: the overlay only appears for a flag, a penalty, DRS or the delta. Always: it stays displayed, showing “TRACK CLEAR” when there is nothing to report. Shared by all sessions.',
  panelInfoTime='Display time for non-urgent information (green flag, start, DRS available, last lap, chequered flag, slippery surface); 0: while it lasts. Flags (yellow, red, blue, neutralisations, penalties) always stay as long as they are active.',
  debugLog='Detailed log: records every AI state change in the diagnostics event log.',
  occLatMin='Lateral occupancy: below this offset, a nearby car is in the same lane (ahead or behind); above it, it occupies a side.',
  occLatMax='Lateral occupancy: beyond this offset, a nearby car no longer occupies the side.',
  occMargin='Longitudinal margin added to the car lengths to judge a side as occupied.',
  occSpeedGain='Extra longitudinal margin per metre per second of closing speed: a car arriving fast occupies the side sooner.',
  gripPerLevelPct='Grip bonus per level point above 100% (0.004 = 0.4% more grip per point). No effect if the grip bonus is not allowed.',
  seed='Personality seed: another value gives other drivers (reactions, courtesy, level offsets). The same seed gives the same personalities again.',
 },
